{"description":"The Guardian article examines how Hershey and Reese’s are reducing cocoa content in their products amid a volatile cocoa market, sparking backlash from a family descendant and highlighting industry strategies such as reformulation and shrinkflation.","articleSummary":"The piece begins with Brad Reese, a descendant of Hershey founder HB Reese, purchasing a newly released Reese’s Unwrapped Peanut Butter Creme Mini Hearts and finding them unpalatable, noting that the product’s coating contains less than 2 % cocoa and is largely sugar and vegetable oil.  Reese’s disappointment led him to investigate other Hershey‑branded confections and discover that several items, including Take 5, Mr Goodbar and Heath bars, also lack milk chocolate.  He publicly criticized Hershey on LinkedIn, questioning how the company could continue to brand itself as a symbol of quality while allegedly replacing key ingredients.  Hershey issued statements defending its product lines and citing “cocoa bean volatility” as a reason for changes.

The article contextualizes the shift by detailing the cocoa market’s turbulence since 2020, driven by droughts and floods in Ghana and Côte d’Ivoire, two of the world’s largest cocoa producers.  Prices spiked from $2,000–$3,000 per ton to $12,000 in 2024, forcing companies to employ strategies such as shrinkflation and reformulation to keep consumer prices stable.  Economists and industry insiders, including Ohio State economist Alexis Villacis and Hershey’s strategic sourcing director Mark Taylor, explain how limited supply, climate risk, and regulatory pressures in the US, UK and EU are prompting manufacturers to experiment with alternative ingredients and “lab‑grown” chocolate substitutes.

Consumers are increasingly scrutinizing ingredient lists, learning that items labeled “chocolate candy” can legally contain below the 15 % cocoa threshold in the US, while EU rules are stricter.  The piece concludes by noting that while premium brands emphasize ethically sourced cocoa and higher cocoa percentages, mass‑market companies like Hershey may continue to replace cocoa as long as sales remain strong, prompting concerns about brand trust and consumer loyalty.","points":["Brad Reese bought Reese’s Unwrapped Peanut Butter Creme Mini Hearts and found the coating contained less than 2 % cocoa, mostly sugar and vegetable oil.","Reese’s open letter to Hershey’s Todd Scott questioned the company’s claim of trust and quality amid ingredient changes.","Hershey has cited volatile cocoa bean markets, with prices reaching $12,000 per ton in 2024, as a reason for reformulation.","The US requires chocolate candy to have at least 15 % cocoa; items below this are still labeled as “chocolate candy,” while EU regulations are stricter.","Economist Alexis Villacis notes that climate-induced supply shocks in Ghana and Côte d’Ivoire have driven up cocoa prices and forced companies to explore alternatives such as lab‑grown substitutes."],"biasSummary":"The article presents a critical viewpoint toward Hershey, foregrounding the voice of a Reese family descendant and industry critics, while giving Hershey limited opportunity to respond beyond issued statements.  It leans toward emphasizing the perceived erosion of brand trust and the economic pressures that may justify ingredient changes, but includes factual context about cocoa market volatility, giving the impression of balanced reporting with a negative slant toward the company’s practices."}

