{
    "description": "A weather report explaining the dramatic temperature swings between winter blizzards and near-record warmth in the United States as it transitions from winter to spring.",
    "articleSummary": "Recent extreme weather in the U.S. has included blizzards, bitter cold, severe storms, and near-record warmth, particularly in March, a typical transition period with large-scale temperature changes. Forecasters attribute this whiplash to an active storm track influencing air masses, a jet stream amplification, and possible climate change effects on the polar vortex. The east saw shifts from heat to snowfall, while the western U.S., including Phoenix and Las Vegas, experienced record-breaking heats and fire risks. The western half of the country remains under a strong heat pattern through late March, while the east will gradually become cooler and wetter.",
