}

{
    "description": "At the 2026 Oscars, a rare live-action short film category tied between 'The Singers' and 'Two People Exchanging Saliva', marking the festival's seventh all-time tie.",
    "articleSummary": "The 2026 Academy Awards featured an unprecedented tie in the live-action short film category, with 'The Singers' and 'Two People Exchanging Saliva' winning. This was the only simultaneous tie in this category in over three decades, following a 2013 sound editing tie and the 1995 live-action short tie. The Oscars announcer, Kumail Nanjiani, humorously acknowledged the rarity of the event, quipping that it was 'a competition, but in the pressroom, [the films] were all smiles.' While the win granted 'The Singers' and 'Two People Exchanging Saliva' their Oscar awards, it also caused temporary disruption, with Oscar presenters and nominees addressing the tie. The Academy’s historical list of all 13 Oscar ties to date is compiled for reference.",
    "points": [
        "The 2026 Oscars had a tie in the best live-action short film category.",
        "The two winning films were 'The Singers' and 'Two People Exchanging Saliva'.",
        "This was the seventh tie in Oscars history and the first in the live-action short category since 2013."
    ],
    "biasSummary": "The article appears neutral, presenting historical facts about the tie and direct quotes from filmmakers and hosts. There is no evident emotional or editorial bias, though host Conan O'Brien’s joke and the comedic tone suggest a more playful reporting style."
}

{"description":"Trump asserts a former president admitted regret over not attacking Iran, yet the former presidents deny recent contact.","articleSummary":"President Donald Trump repeatedly claimed that he had elicited a confession from a former U.S. president who expressed regret for not acting against Iran, stating the conversation took place in a private setting. Trump withheld the identity of the former president, citing concerns over embarrassing the individual, and said he had spoken to the ex‑president in a meeting he held at the White House while opening a Kennedy Center trustees’ session. Representatives for all four living former presidents—Bill Clinton, George W. Bush, Barack Obama and Joe Biden—responded that none had been in contact with Trump in recent times, and the White House declined to comment. Trump used the occasion to reaffirm his hard‑line stance on Iran, claiming no previous president had the courage to confront the threat, while also praising Clinton and criticizing Biden and Obama for their perceived weakness on the issue.

The story also noted Trump’s broader political attacks on Biden and Obama, labeling Biden the “worst president” and accusing Obama of negotiating a “horrible deal” with Iran, a criticism he had levied during his first term. He announced a task force to eliminate fraud in federal benefit programs, led by Vice President JD Vance, in a speech held in the Oval Office.

